:: TeachYoungerChild [childhood]
# Passing on Knowledge

You notice a younger child watching your stone game with curious eyes. Though you're still learning yourself, you decide to teach what you know to this smaller child.

"Would you like to learn how to play?" you ask.

The little one nods shyly and sits down beside you. You begin by arranging the stones in the simplest pattern and explaining the basic rules.

"You can move one stone at a time, like this," you demonstrate. "And you can only move to an empty space. If you jump over my stone, you capture it."

Teaching, you discover, requires you to understand the game even better than when you're just playing. You must break down each concept into simple parts that a younger child can grasp. When the little one makes mistakes, you must explain patiently why certain moves don't work.

The older children who taught you are watching with approval. In your tribe, knowledge flows from older to younger members - each generation teaching the next. By taking on this teaching role, even in a small way, you're participating in this important tradition.

By the end of your lesson, the younger child has grasped the basics of the game and is beaming with pride at this new accomplishment.

